import{_ as t,o as s,c as a,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.CNDaxUod.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Web app","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/web-app.md","filePath":"guide/web-app.md"}'),o={name:"guide/web-app.md"};function r(i,e,d,h,l,p){return s(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="web-app" tabindex="-1">Web app <a class="header-anchor" href="#web-app" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Web app&quot;">​</a></h1><p>AsyncUp&#39;s console lives at <strong><code>https://HOST/app</code></strong>. It is a single-page app served by the same process as the Chat bot and the JSON API, so there is nothing extra to deploy.</p><h2 id="signing-in" tabindex="-1">Signing in <a class="header-anchor" href="#signing-in" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Signing in&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The sign-in page offers whichever methods are configured:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Who lands where</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Continue with Google</strong></td><td>Workspace admins get the console; everyone else gets their own page</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Continue with SSO</strong> (SAML)</td><td>Same split, with the admin role taken from the IdP group attribute</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Operator token</strong> (<code>OPERATOR_TOKEN</code>)</td><td>The console, acting as the workspace operator. Kept in the browser tab only; switch it off under <em>Settings › Sign-in &amp; SSO</em> once a real method works</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Setting these up: <a href="./enterprise-sso.html">Sign-in</a>.</p><p>On a fresh install an admin lands in the <strong>guided setup</strong> (<code>/app/setup</code>): Cloud project → service account → Chat app configuration with a live check for the first signed event → optional sign-in → the first standup from a template, run immediately. Progress is saved as you go; the welcome page detects a working Chat connection and skips straight to the standup step. <a href="./first-standup.html">Guided setup and first test</a> walks through it.</p><h2 id="what-s-there" tabindex="-1">What&#39;s there <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-s-there" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What&#39;s there&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Standups</strong> — stats strip (submitted today, participation, open blockers, missing), the table, and per standup: <strong>Overview</strong> (today&#39;s run polled live, recent runs, open blockers, schedule), <strong>History</strong> (every run with full answers, CSV export, and mood comments for people allowed to read them), <strong>Insights</strong> (participation by person, mood and blocker charts), <strong>Polls</strong> (polls with questions: open and closed, <strong>New poll</strong> for managers, and each poll&#39;s results, CSV export and <strong>Close now</strong>) and <strong>Settings</strong> (schedule with time pickers, questions, mood, the People grid and roster group, escalation, webhook with a signed test, digest, and a Danger zone to archive, unarchive or delete).</li><li><strong>Blockers</strong> — every open, acknowledged or resolved blocker across the standups you can see, with filters and the same actions as in Chat.</li><li><strong>Reports</strong> — participation, team mood and blockers over 4, 8 or 12 weeks, for all standups or one.</li><li><strong>Team</strong> — everyone on a roster with role, standups, timezone and status; roster changes with confirmation.</li><li><strong>Settings</strong> — General (default timezone, Calendar out-of-office, leave in Chat and Calendar with how its event declines invites and the latest failure, and spelling suggestions with the English and words to accept), Google Chat (with live verification), Sign-in &amp; SSO, MCP server, API &amp; tokens, Roles, Access, Danger zone (disconnect Chat; delete all history — both behind a typed confirmation). Each section shows only to people with its permission.</li><li><strong>My standups</strong> (<code>/app/me</code>) — for everyone else: today&#39;s standup with an <em>Answer in Chat</em> link and <em>Skip today</em> (which asks for a one-line reason), recent answers, timezone, vacation mode and <em>My schedule</em>.</li></ul><p>Admins see the whole workspace. <strong>Managers</strong>, the people who administer a standup, see and manage their own standups, blockers, reports and team without Workspace-admin rights. A <strong>Viewer</strong> reads the same pages and changes nothing. Everyone else gets the personal page. Pages, tabs and buttons follow each person&#39;s permissions, including custom roles (<a href="./roles.html">Roles and permissions</a>).</p><h2 id="people" tabindex="-1">People <a class="header-anchor" href="#people" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;People&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A standup&#39;s <strong>Settings → People</strong> grid lists everyone in its Chat space, or everyone in the Google Group it follows (<a href="./directory.html">Directory and group rosters</a>). Per person:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>On standup</td><td>Prompted for this standup</td></tr><tr><td>Required</td><td>Listed as missing when they don&#39;t answer. The others still get the prompt but never count as missing</td></tr><tr><td>Reports to</td><td>A manager who can also approve their days off on this standup, without managing it; <em>None</em> by default. Only people who manage a standup are offered. The create form doesn&#39;t show it</td></tr><tr><td>Mon–Sun</td><td>The days they work on this standup. The standup&#39;s days are marked <em>default</em>; any other day can be ticked, and the standup then runs that day for them. Each row says <em>Default</em> or <em>Custom</em> (with <em>Reset to default</em>), and each tick saves at once and shows <em>Saved</em>. Unticked days mean no prompt, and never missing</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Roster</strong> switches between <em>Picked from the space</em> and <em>Everyone in a Google Group</em>. Either way, <strong>Last sync</strong> shows when the roster was last checked, with <strong>Sync now</strong> and the last error.</p><ul><li><em>Picked from the space</em>: anyone who leaves the space comes off the standup, and comes back with their old settings if they rejoin. <strong>Last sync</strong> lists who is off because they left, with the date. Someone you take off by hand stays off, even if they rejoin.</li><li><em>Everyone in a Google Group</em>: people you untick stay off (<a href="./directory.html">Directory and group rosters</a>).</li></ul><p>The create form uses the same grid, with everyone in the space ticked to start.</p><p>People&#39;s days on each standup, days off and time-off approvals also live under <strong>Team → Edit schedule</strong>, <strong>My page → My schedule</strong> and <strong>Team → Requests</strong> — see <a href="./schedules.html">Personal schedules</a>.</p><p><strong>Team → Requests</strong> also lists requests to edit answers after the deadline, for anyone who can configure the standup: who asked, which standup and day, their reason, and when the request lapses (midnight in the standup&#39;s timezone). <strong>Approve</strong> lets them edit until then; <strong>Decline…</strong> takes an optional note sent to them. The first decision wins, in Chat or here.</p><h2 id="naming-archiving-and-deleting" tabindex="-1">Naming, archiving and deleting <a class="header-anchor" href="#naming-archiving-and-deleting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Naming, archiving and deleting&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Standup names are unique across the workspace, archived standups included, ignoring case. The create form flags a clash before you press Create.</li><li><strong>Archive</strong> (Danger zone, type <code>ARCHIVE</code>) stops prompts and keeps history. <strong>Unarchive</strong> resumes on the next scheduled day.</li><li><strong>Delete</strong> removes an archived standup with every run, answer, blocker and poll, after you type its name. Only workspace admins can delete. Messages already in Chat stay, and people&#39;s own weeks and days off are kept.</li></ul><h2 id="security-notes" tabindex="-1">Security notes <a class="header-anchor" href="#security-notes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Security notes&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Browser sessions are signed cookies (<code>SECRET_KEY</code>); state-changing API calls from the app carry an <code>X-Requested-With</code> header as CSRF proof.</li><li>The operator token is only ever sent as a bearer header and is never stored server-side beyond the environment variable.</li><li>Secrets (service-account key, OAuth secret, SAML certificate) are encrypted at rest and never echoed back by the API — the app only ever learns that they are set.</li><li>The API and the app share one authorisation model; see the <a href="./api.html">API guide</a> for the exact rules per endpoint.</li></ul>',22)])])}const u=t(o,[["render",r]]);export{g as __pageData,u as default};
